1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:01,977
Hypersonic missiles.

2
00:00:02,327 --> 00:00:07,226
How does a missile that flies more than five times the speed of sound actually work? Let's take a look.

3
00:00:09,276 --> 00:00:14,303
Mach one is the speed of sound: about twelve hundred thirty five kilometers per hour at sea level.

4
00:00:14,653 --> 00:00:19,541
An airliner cruises at Mach zero point eight five, and Concorde flew at Mach two.

5
00:00:19,891 --> 00:00:25,278
Hypersonic missiles go beyond Mach five, that is, more than six thousand kilometers per hour.

6
00:00:25,628 --> 00:00:29,866
At that speed, London to Paris would take only about three and a half minutes.

7
00:00:31,916 --> 00:00:34,435
The first type is the hypersonic glide vehicle.

8
00:00:34,785 --> 00:00:38,628
First, a rocket booster carries the vehicle very high and very fast.

9
00:00:38,978 --> 00:00:41,300
Then the glide vehicle separates from the rocket.

10
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:46,619
It glides through the upper atmosphere at Mach five to twenty, skipping and maneuvering as it goes.

11
00:00:46,969 --> 00:00:50,046
Unlike a ballistic missile, its path is hard to predict.

12
00:00:52,096 --> 00:00:54,870
The second type is the scramjet powered cruise missile.

13
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:59,365
The inlet compresses incoming air using shock waves. There are no moving parts.

14
00:00:59,715 --> 00:01:04,673
In the combustor, fuel is burned inside air that is still flowing at supersonic speed.

15
00:01:05,023 --> 00:01:08,691
The expanding hot gas exits through the nozzle and produces thrust.

16
00:01:09,041 --> 00:01:14,428
Because a scramjet only works above about Mach four, the missile is first accelerated by a rocket.

17
00:01:16,478 --> 00:01:19,067
The biggest enemy of a hypersonic missile is heat.

18
00:01:19,417 --> 00:01:24,189
Air compression and friction heat the surface to more than two thousand degrees Celsius.

19
00:01:24,539 --> 00:01:29,125
A layer of plasma forms around the missile, which disrupts radio communication.

20
00:01:29,475 --> 00:01:33,736
That's why engineers use carbon carbon composites and ceramic heat shields.

21
00:01:35,786 --> 00:01:37,632
So why are they so hard to stop?

22
00:01:37,982 --> 00:01:41,488
A ballistic missile climbs high, so radar can see it early.

23
00:01:41,838 --> 00:01:45,739
A hypersonic missile flies low, staying below the radar horizon.

24
00:01:46,089 --> 00:01:48,759
It keeps maneuvering, so its path can't be predicted.

25
00:01:49,109 --> 00:01:52,488
Only a few minutes remain between detection and impact.

26
00:01:54,538 --> 00:02:00,958
In short, hypersonic missiles combine extreme speed with maneuverability, making them very hard to defend against.

27
00:02:01,308 --> 00:02:02,179
Thanks for watching.
